faq:
  - q: "How is this different from a one-day AI workshop?"
    a: "A workshop teaches awareness; three months changes behaviour. Practice staff leave a workshop interested and are back to writing letters the old way inside a fortnight, because nobody built the habit on real correspondence. Our program runs across three months on your practice's own work, and the ongoing support keeps it current as the tools and the rules move."
  - q: "Is this safe with patient data?"
    a: "With the right tool tier and the right workflow, which is exactly what the training establishes. Safety here is a function of setup and habit rather than of the tool alone — and most practices currently have neither while staff use these tools anyway. The program closes that gap and leaves you with a policy."
  - q: "What does it cost?"
    a: "It's quoted per team size, and the three months is a fixed cost rather than an open-ended engagement, so you know the total before you start. Tell us who you'd send and we'll give you the number, along with the tasks we'd expect to compress first."
  - q: "Can AI write clinical notes?"
    a: "Dedicated clinical scribe products exist and some practices use them — that's a separate purchase with its own compliance questions. Our program builds the practice's own capability across administration, correspondence and patient communication, which is the larger and far less served part of the workload."
  - q: "Who in the practice should do this?"
    a: "Practice managers and reception get the most immediate return, because their work is highest in volume and lowest in clinical risk. Clinicians usually join for the correspondence and patient-explanation modules. You don't need to send everyone."
  - q: "We're a dental practice, not a GP clinic. Does it apply?"
    a: "Yes. Dental, allied health, specialist and veterinary practices share the same administrative shape — referrals, recalls, patient explanation and policy upkeep. The examples are adjusted to the practices in the cohort."
  - q: "How long before we see anything?"
    a: "Correspondence drafting and patient explanations usually land in the first fortnight, because they're frequent and immediately noticeable. The larger workflow changes take the full three months to bed in."
